ReadArchiveBinsafe: bound the hash table's entry count.

`ReadArchiveBinsafe::read_header` reads the table's entry count straight off the
file and sizes the entry vector with it:

	auto hash_table_size = read->read_uint();
	_m_hash_table_entries.resize(hash_table_size);

	for (std::uint32_t i = 0; i < hash_table_size; ++i) { ... }

The same shape patches 0034, 0036, 0037, 0040 and 0042 bound elsewhere, and the
loop cannot stop on its own for the same reason: every read past the end of the
file returns zero, so a zero `keyLength` and a zero `insertionIndex` satisfy
patch 0029's bound and the loop runs to the file's own count, once per unit.

Measured on the unpatched reader against `zenkit-node`'s 1.4 KB fixture with the
count rewritten in place to 0x0FFFFFFF: a **20.5 GB peak working set**, and the
world still reports LOADED after 35 s. An absurd count is the harmless one here
too, exactly as in 0037 and 0042 -- 0xFFFFFFFF is 4 billion entries, `resize`
throws `bad_alloc` and the loop is never entered, in 69 ms -- so the dangerous
value is the merely large one and a test seeded with 0xFFFFFFFF would pass
against the unpatched reader.

Bounded by the bytes actually left in the file, with the same `_bytes_remaining`
helper as `Mesh.cc`, `world/BspTree.cc`, `world/WayNet.cc` and `vobs/Camera.cc`.
Eight bytes is the smallest an entry can be on the wire -- two `uint16`s and a
`uint32` before a key that may legitimately be empty -- so the bound never
refuses a world the engine wrote.

This is patch 0029's function and patch 0029's chunk, and 0029 bounded the other
count in it: the `insertionIndex` that *indexes* this vector. It did not bound
the count that *sizes* it.

Not found by `zenkit-node/tools/fuzz-world.js --counts`, and it could not have
been: the sweep rewrites the INTEGER *entries* of the entry stream, and this is a
raw `uint32` in the container's own hash-table header, which no entry walk
reaches. Read that as the sweep's second documented limit, beside the one patches
0040 and 0042 named -- a field the fixture does not carry as an entry is a field
the sweep cannot reach, whether it is missing or merely not an entry.

diff --git a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
--- a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
@@ -11,6 +11,23 @@
 #include <cstring>
 
 namespace zenkit {
+	// The number of bytes left in the reader. `Read` has no size, but every
+	// implementation can seek to the end and back. Same helper, and the same
+	// reason for it, as the ones in `Mesh.cc`, `world/BspTree.cc`, `world/WayNet.cc`
+	// and `vobs/Camera.cc`.
+	static std::size_t _bytes_remaining(Read* r) {
+		auto here = r->tell();
+		r->seek(0, Whence::END);
+		auto end = r->tell();
+		r->seek(static_cast<ssize_t>(here), Whence::BEG);
+		return end > here ? end - here : 0;
+	}
+
+	// A hash table entry is at least this many bytes on the wire: `keyLength` and
+	// `insertionIndex` as `uint16`s and `hashValue` as a `uint32`, before the key
+	// itself, which may legitimately be empty.
+	static constexpr std::size_t HASH_TABLE_ENTRY_MIN_BYTES = 8;
+
 	void ReadArchiveBinsafe::read_header() {
 		_m_bs_version = read->read_uint();
 		_m_object_count = read->read_uint();
@@ -21,6 +38,22 @@
